{
  "dom_id": "step-q66087",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "What is the value of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4$ at its local minimum points?",
  "worked_solution": "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and adding $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 2+4 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 6 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at its local minimum points.",
